From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: punkratz1 <punkratz1@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 16:33:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 10156: ARM: Return 0 from SDIV/UDIV on divide by zero

Per the ARM ARM, SDIV and UDIV write 0 to Rd when the divisor is 0.
P-code division by zero is undefined, so guard the division like the
AArch64 sdiv/udiv constructors do. This fixes the A32 and T32 forms;
previously SDIV faulted in the p-code emulator and UDIV faulted in the
JIT emulator. UDIV no longer widens to 8 bytes, since an unsigned
32-bit division cannot overflow.

Add emulation tests covering divide by zero, normal division,
truncation toward zero, INT_MIN / -1, unsigned operands, Rd aliasing
Rm and conditional execution, for A32 and T32, on both the p-code and
the JIT emulator.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MTr23xzr4mibyzERoo5SgS
